<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#leysingi">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leysingi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as leysingr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In Norse law, a leysingr (plural leysingi) was a freed slave, or freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thrall</a>. A slave whose freedom had been given but not made public was a frjálsgjafar, the lowest rank of freedman, who became a leysingi by giving a freedom ale; manumission had to be confirmed by oath and announced at an assembly or in church. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">Freedmen</a> stayed inferior in the law codes: the former master controlled where they lived and whom they married, they could not leave his property without permission, and their children's inheritance was restricted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a>, most widespread in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, was small-scale and household-based, and it had disappeared in most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> by the 14th century.</p></div>
